WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.160
How to go from small talk to deep&nbsp;
conversation? I'm Chad and let's get started.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.440
So, through my work I've gotten to facilitate&nbsp;
thousands of conversations with hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:29.040
thousands of people about all sorts of topics.&nbsp;
And in that work, I've learned a couple really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.360
key lessons that have helped me create more&nbsp;
conversations that matter. And so in this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.000
I'm going to unpack and start with sharing&nbsp;
a story about Amanda, student in a program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.480
that I was facilitating- training that I was&nbsp;
facilitating, at San Jose State University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:49.200
2 days ago actually. And I'm gonna&nbsp;
share the relearning or "aha" that I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.840
in relation to how do you take conversations&nbsp;
from small talk, which very few people like,&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:00.960
to real talk, to deeper conversation in a&nbsp;
comfortable, natural way. And so in this video, I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:08.000
going to share this main paradigm that I learned.&nbsp;
But first, Amanda, so I'm facilitating this program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.360
and Amanda unmuted- it was virtual and Amanda&nbsp;
unmuted and shared something about herself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.520
and I realized she only spoke for about 5&nbsp;
seconds. But, in that 5 seconds, I asked the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.160
of the group, I asked, "Amanda, are you okay being put&nbsp;
on the spot for a minute?" And I asked the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.240
of the group, "What did we just learn about Amanda?"&nbsp;
And even though she only spoke for 5 seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:32.160
we were able to come up with more than 5&nbsp;
things that we learned about Amanda, and yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:38.640
most people, in that setting, the conversation would&nbsp;
have just fell flat, and yet in my mind, one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.760
most powerful tools to go from small talk to&nbsp;
real talk or small talk to deeper conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.960
is to follow that curiosity, but in a very specific&nbsp;
direction which I'll explain in a moment. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.040
a big part of my job is inviting people to stretch&nbsp;
outside of their comfort zone, I believe that every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.080
year, I need to do at least 1 big thing that&nbsp;
stretches me outside of my own comfort zone. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:04.080
last year, I was on a 10-day kayak camping retreat&nbsp;
in the middle of the woods, no self-service for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:10.160
over a week in total wilderness. Paddling from&nbsp;
site to site where we were camping and there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.720
was a lot of alone time. There was a lot of time&nbsp;
to think and an "aha" showed up for me which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:21.200
this graph that we're gonna put up right here.&nbsp;
So, from a spectrum of small talk over here over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.720
to real talk over here, you're watching this video&nbsp;
because you want to be on this side of the graph.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.960
Now, to add 1 dimension to this, I would say&nbsp;
the most powerful tool to move from one place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:37.360
to the other is curiosity. And so, we draw another&nbsp;
line coming down from a level of high curiosity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:45.200
to a level of low curiosity. The tool that I would&nbsp;
love for you to employ is follow your vertical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:51.680
curiosity. And what I mean by that is go up with&nbsp;
your curiosity as opposed to what most of us do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:57.120
is horizontal curiosity shopping. So, we kind&nbsp;
of like bounce from topic to topic to topic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.880
as opposed to going really deep and intentional&nbsp;
with 1 thought by listening very deeply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.080
and continuing to be curious about that element.&nbsp;
Kind of circling down that vertical curiosity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:15.200
is the easiest tool that you can just&nbsp;
choose to do to go from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:22.800
small talk to real talk. Now, let me be really&nbsp;
clear, for me the definition, as far as small talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.520
has nothing to do with the topic of conversation.&nbsp;
So, typically when we think about small talk we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:33.360
think about the weather or where you're from or&nbsp;
what do you do. I would say that small talk has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.240
everything to do with the process and the way that&nbsp;
you're having the conversation. 2 meteorologists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:43.200
talking about the weather might not be small&nbsp;
talk. It might be a really good conversation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:50.640
And so, we can't eliminate or judge whether a topic&nbsp;
is worthy of being called "deeper conversation"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:57.520
just by the topic of conversation. And so, the&nbsp;
process really is following- that makes real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:03.520
conversation happen, is following your genuine&nbsp;
curiosity vertically, not horizontally and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:09.200
also listening. Curiosity and great questions are&nbsp;
worthless without these 2 appendages on the side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:15.520
of our head that allow us to retain information,&nbsp;
understand it and potentially spit it back because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:21.280
when we listen really well, we can get curious&nbsp;
about many more things. And so in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.720
going back to who we'll call Amanda from San Jose&nbsp;
State University, the piece that she shared she-&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:31.920
asked everybody to go grab an object that had&nbsp;
some meaning to them and she brought back this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.800
little tiny frame with a constellation in it. And&nbsp;
she just shared, "I'm not really into zodiac signs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:42.480
but I'm a cancer." And she said like maybe 1 other&nbsp;
phrase and that was it. There was no other context.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:47.520
And yet when I asked the group, "what did you learn&nbsp;
from Amanda?" They were able to come up with 5 or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.520
more things, right. She's kind into zodiac but&nbsp;
not really, but knows enough to be dangerous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.480
She's a cancer, she's a comfortable enough to&nbsp;
share in front of the group, right. So, now we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:02.560
start to travel into things that she didn't say&nbsp;
verbatim, but were present in the actual group.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.760
And so, there's some massive value in thing in&nbsp;
realizing that if you listen really intentionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.440
what you're curious about will change because what&nbsp;
you hear will actually fundamentally be different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.720
I've unpacked this in a couple other videos&nbsp;
about how to start conversations with new people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.960
that you can check out right here, but one of my- by&nbsp;
far my favorite features of conversation is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.480
idea that dialogue is exponential. Conversation&nbsp;
and connection is exponential and here's exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:37.840
what I mean by that. So, if I learn about this&nbsp;
plant, right. If I learn that it used to grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:45.680
in a greenhouse 15 minutes away, if that's&nbsp;
all I learned about this plant, right, I can ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.840
who works at that greenhouse, who started that&nbsp;
greenhouse, what's the story behind it, what does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:56.080
the owner care about, right. We could go on for- this&nbsp;
video could be unnecessarily long coming up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:02.800
questions about the story of just a plant. Human&nbsp;
beings are way more complex and so, when you- when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.880
somebody speaks for 10 seconds, almost certainly&nbsp;
you can learn at least 3 things from them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.800
And then if you ask a question about any of these&nbsp;
3 things, you'll learn 3 more things. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:19.920
then you ask a question about any of those and&nbsp;
you've got 3 more topics. And so, you can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:25.040
how conversation is exponential and yet typically,&nbsp;
I think this is really important because with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:30.080
people that we're close to in our personal lives,&nbsp;
but also especially colleagues in our work lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:35.600
we follow like one curiosity path a little bit. We&nbsp;
go horizontal curiosity. We kind of like learn all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:42.160
the facts about somebody right here, but we never&nbsp;
get past like 5 layers deep of curiosity. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:45.840
so, you're watching this video because you want&nbsp;
to go from small talk to deeper conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.240
this is the way to do it. Break past that barrier&nbsp;
because usually we go about 3 layers deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:55.440
in curiosity and then we put somebody in a box and&nbsp;
they stay in that box and our curiosity dies. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:01.440
you know, just before recording actually I was&nbsp;
leading a workshop for Rutgers University and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:06.080
somebody asked a question about when you work&nbsp;
with colleagues for 10 plus years you feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:11.600
you know everything about them, even though&nbsp;
realistically you only know 2 percent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:16.000
their world and yet you put them in a box.&nbsp;
You don't- if you work somebody for 10 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.960
it was probably in the 1st year that they landed&nbsp;
in the box. It was either like, "He's the funny guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.800
he's always a jokester." "He's the curmudgeon who's&nbsp;
always cranky and has nothing positive to say in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:31.440
a meeting." We put people in these boxes and&nbsp;
that limits or puts a ceiling on our curiosity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.760
and prevents that vertical curiosity from&nbsp;
piercing through. And I would say to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:40.960
a personal note from this when Will, my&nbsp;
co-founder and partner, really taught me this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:50.000
When my curiosity is not present with Kate,&nbsp;
my wife, our relationship is dead, right .When I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:56.480
am not curious about who Kate is there is no&nbsp;
progression, there's no relationship moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:02.160
forward in that sense. The coolest part though is&nbsp;
that curiosity is a choice. It's a choice and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:08.560
also kind of like a muscle. The more you use it the&nbsp;
more easy it is to use. The more curious you are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.880
the more easy it is to be curious. There's this&nbsp;
like awesome cyclical effect. But you're watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:17.280
this video to go from small talk to deeper&nbsp;
conversation and so really all you have to do is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:24.960
be curious 3 times today and then 3 times&nbsp;
tomorrow, and the more days you go on being curious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.920
following that curiosity with questions, the deeper&nbsp;
conversations you'll find yourself having. And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:34.800
you're asking rather than random curiosities from&nbsp;
your brain, if you're asking about what somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:40.400
has just shared with you from what you've listened,
the conversation will very quickly move from small

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:47.040
talk to real talk. If you enjoyed this video,&nbsp;
it turns out that my job is helping people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:51.840
create conversations that matter. Will and I wrote&nbsp;
a book about it it's called "Ask Powerful Questions:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:56.320
Create Conversations That Matter." There's a link&nbsp;
below with a free excerpt from it. There's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:01.680
this thing called Amazon, I've heard of it recently,&nbsp;
that actually carries books, believe it or not. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:07.680
sell them on the internet, it's amazing. Awesome&nbsp;
hanging out on cyberspace with you. Have an awesome day.

